One of the more reliable warning signs is when junk bond traders flee before stock market investors catch on.

It's not like it predicts a crash or anything; just a generally tough market environment until the junk bond A/D line starts rising again. https://t.co/OJetug62Yo

@CarloFerlauto The lines are not arbitrary. They objectively show the dates when the S&amp;P 500 was within several % of a high and the junk bond A/D line was at a 52-week low. Feel free to test the data yourself, it's all publicly available.

@CarloFerlauto Valid questions, and ones that anyone should ask when they see a backtest. I used 5% drawdown for SPY (within what is typically considered pullback territory) and a standard 52-week low for the A/D line.
